explanation:
Recursive chunking's core idea is hierarchical fallback splitting, described in the notes as 'smart scissors': try the largest natural boundary first (paragraph breaks, 

); if the resulting chunk from that split is still bigger than chunk_size, recurse into that oversized chunk and try the next boundary down (sentence/line breaks, 
); if still too big, try word boundaries; and only as an absolute last resort, cut at the character level — with recursion going 'only as deep as needed per chunk,' meaning some chunks might stop at the paragraph level while others recurse all the way to character-level cuts, depending on how large the original content block was. This preserves semantic meaning far better than pure length-based chunking because it always prefers the most natural, meaning-preserving boundary that still satisfies the size constraint. Option A misrepresents the process as simultaneous application of all separators, when it's actually a sequential, conditional fallback — each level is only tried if the previous level's result was insufficient. Option C and D both invert the documented priority order, which explicitly starts at the LARGEST natural boundary (paragraphs) and only falls back to progressively smaller units (sentences → words → characters) as needed, never the reverse.
